# SYNOPSIS

```text
userboot.next=bin/launcher launcher.until=board-test
```

# DESCRIPTION

board-test is the board image's last service: the image a board is booted with to see that it is stable.
The image holds every service, the console and its shell among them, with fs(4) on an NVMe disk's `taisce` partition, the first of `/dev/sdN0` and `/dev/sdN1` to have one (on the Orange Pi 6 Plus its second disk; its first is FreeBSD's), a volume made there the first time, but not the test programs or the launcher's own test.
Its cpu image, `bootfs-board-cpu.img`, is the same with a cpu server (cpu(8)) for this terminal, running for good: the board's end of the bring-up link (vx-cpu(1), vx-remote(1)'s `--image board-cpu`).
Its desktop image, `bootfs-board-desktop.img`, is the cpu image with the desktop's programs: radharc(4) on the display a display driver lit (on the Orange Pi 6 Plus, linlon(3)'s HDMI output), rio, its apps and the workspaces, running for good.
The launcher runs until board-test ends and exits with its code (launcher(8)); the board image's command line then reboots, so a board whose firmware boots the image once returns to its own system.

It waits up to 30 seconds for devmgr(8) to bind its drivers, then checks that every device devmgr bound has its driver running, a PCI function's with its DMA on an IOMMU (not `passthrough` or `none`; an ACPI device that firmware puts behind no IOMMU, as the Orange Pi 6 Plus's USB controllers, may be `passthrough`), that every IOMMU devmgr names is `ok`, and that each disk at `/dev/sd`* answers its `ctl` with a `geometry` line, which needs the driver to have read the disk (its partition table) through its DMA.
It writes a MiB to `/data/board-test`, removing the last run's first and waiting up to 10 seconds for fs(4) to serve the volume, and checks that it reads back the same.
It checks that each USB controller's xhci(3) has looked at the devices on its bus (`bus ready`); in QEMU, whose USB keyboards and mice are vendor `0627`'s, it then plugs a keyboard into the hub there (`qemu: device_add`), checks that xhci finds the hub, the mouse on it and the keyboard, types a key on it (`qemu: sendkey a`), and checks that xhci gave kbdfs(4) its press and release.
It checks that the SCMI agent, scmi(3), where devmgr runs one, answers on each channel it has, and that each GPU's mali(3) powered the GPU up and read its GPU_ID (`probed`) and booted its command-stream firmware (`firmware booted`), waiting up to 10 seconds.
It checks that power(8) asked every CPU whose `_CPC` names a register in memory for its highest level, or set every CPU in the devicetree's `cpus` lines at its fastest point, or says there is no CPPC, and that every thermal zone devmgr(8) lists reads its temperature and is one power reads.
With devicetree CPUs it then sets each of their clocks, through its driver's `ctl`, to their slowest point and back to their fastest, the supply left at the fastest's voltage, and checks that a chain of five million dependent multiplies takes as many times longer at the slowest as the fastest is faster, within a tenth: the clocks move, and the fastest runs at its rate.
Where there is a GPU it then powers it off and on through devmgr's `ctl` (`power <mali service> off`, then `on`, devmgr(8)): off within 10 seconds, with its supplies ended and each provider cut, then on within 30 seconds, powered up again in its order and its driver started again, which must read the GPU_ID it read the first time (`board-test: ok: the GPU powered off and on`).
Then it starts gpu-test(8) through the launcher's `ctl` and waits up to 30 seconds for the launcher to report it stopped (`board-test: ok: gpu-test ran on the GPU and ended`); gpu-test's own checks are its `gpu-test: ok:` lines on the console.
The console on the display is not touched.
Where there is none, as in QEMU, it says `no GPU here: the power cycle is skipped` and no check is made.
It checks that each display engine's linlon(3) probed its engine or left it unlit, and that firmware lit exactly one; it then shows on that one's output eight colour bars, white to black, for two seconds, and firmware's buffer again, and checks that each flip latched (`timeouts 0`).
Then it waits up to 90 seconds from its start for `/net/ether0`'s link to come up and for ip(4) to take an IPv4 address on it, by DHCP or as configured, which needs frames to have gone both ways.
It prints a line for each check, `board-test: ok:` or `board-test: FAILED:` and what it checks, each bound device's line of devmgr's status, each IOMMU, each disk's `ctl`, the interface's status, and at the end each driver's own `status`; its last line is `board-test: passed` or `board-test: FAILED, <n> checks`.
It exits 0 if every check passed.

# A RUN THAT ENDS ITSELF

A board whose image can't be reached to reboot it, because the launcher goes on after board-test ends (the Orange Pi RV2's boot, which doesn't end when it does) or because something wedged, would need a power cycle.
With `croi.watchdog_limit=SECONDS` on the kernel's command line the kernel stops refreshing the watchdog that long into the boot, so the board resets one watchdog timeout (30 seconds) after its last refresh.
Just before that, at the limit, it writes the stuck report to the debuglog, which the console shows: a line for each process (its koid, name and job's name) and under it a line for each of its threads, with its state and, for a blocked one, what it waits on: the syscall it is in, the object its handle names (`port_wait on port 1203 (handle 0x1a3)`), a futex's address, or a pager's page fault; and when the wait times out or that it has no timeout.
Kernel threads follow, by name.
The report is one thread's look at the kernel, a thread at a time: a lock it can't take it says is busy and goes on without it, or stops, saying so.
Where there is no watchdog, as in QEMU, the limit still brings the report, and nothing resets the machine.

The board image with a process that never ends, `bin/wedge` (a thread `wedge-port` waits on a port nothing queues to), wedges a run on purpose: `esp-board-wedge` runs board-test as usual and the launcher until the wedge ends, with a limit of 150 seconds, so the board's console ends with the report naming `wedge-port` and the board resets by itself.

